# Spec Writing (Multi-Perspective Spec Model)

This project uses a **multi-perspective spec model**: every spec captures input from all relevant roles in one document. Required sections are enforced — a spec cannot be marked approved if a required section is empty. Optional sections are filled only when relevant.

See `docs/SPEC-MODEL.md` for the full structure.

## Format

Use the template at `specs/_template.md`. Specs have six parts:

| Part | Sections |
|---|---|
| **1. Intent** | Business [REQUIRED], Functional [REQUIRED], Out of scope [REQUIRED] |
| **2. User experience** | Design (if UI), Accessibility [REQUIRED if UI] |
| **3. Non-functional** | Security [REQUIRED], Privacy [REQUIRED if personal data], Performance, SEO, Analytics, Localization |
| **4. Technical** | Technical (architecture, integrations, implementation constraints) |
| **5. Validation & delivery** | Testing [REQUIRED], Documentation [REQUIRED], Observability, Deployment |
| **6. Meta** | Clarifications [REQUIRED], References |

## Required sections

Every spec must fill these (a section can be filled with concrete content, "Standard project [area] applies", or "Not applicable: [reason]"):

- **Always required**: Business, Functional, Out of scope, Security, Testing, Documentation, Clarifications
- **Conditionally required**: Accessibility (if `feature-type: ui` or `mixed`), Privacy (if `personal-data: yes`)

## Documentation section split

The Documentation section has two subsections:

- **Pre-implementable docs** — admin guides, API contracts, end-user copy defaults, SDK READMEs. Written before code via the docs phase. Drives implementation thinking; updated deliberately when implementation reveals reality differs.
- **Post-implementable docs** — JSDoc, runbooks needing real metrics, troubleshooting from real failure modes. Backfilled after code.

Both subsections must be filled or marked Not applicable.

## Rules

- Business requirements only in Business and Functional — no code, file paths, or implementation details
- Implementation-level decisions go in the Technical section, sparingly
- Every acceptance criterion must be testable by an automated test
- User-facing text must match the application's language
- For modifications: clearly mark what changed vs what stays the same; the git diff scopes the test, doc, and implementation work that follows
- Set status to `draft` — only the user can approve specs
- Frontmatter must include `feature-type` and `personal-data` (these drive conditional-required logic)

## After approval

Remind to commit the spec before starting tests, docs, or code. The `git diff` of the spec commit scopes everything downstream. The full workflow is: spec → tests → docs → implement → review → ship.
